  <div class="abstract">
    <p data-label="Summary">Post is the page where the results of the workflows are finished. It follows the layer model
    of image editors such as GIMP: the result of each workflow, or of any block, is a layer, with an opacity, a blend mode
    and a mask. Each adjustment is a filter on its layer, which can be switched off, changed or removed on its own at any
    time. Every workflow ends with an Output block, which brings its linear image to the screen with an automatic
    stretch; Post shows that stretch with a few sliders, from 0 to 4, and the filters go on top.</p>
  </div>

  <ol class="chain" aria-label="Position in the graph">
    <li><span>Color</span></li><li><span>Stars/background</span></li>
    <li class="here"><span>Post</span></li><li><span>Report</span></li>
  </ol>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s1"><span class="secnum">1</span>The Output block, and what Post receives</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>The sky.</b> The level removed is the one the <a href="/blocks/gradient/">Gradient</a> block measured between the objects. A
    median over the whole field would take a nebula that fills it for sky, differently in each colour.</aside>
    <p>The Output block ends every workflow. It is automatic and cannot be cut. It takes the last image of the chain, the
    denoised stack if the workflow has <a href="/blocks/denoise/">Denoise</a>, else the stack with its gradient removed, and writes it as a linear image
    in display colours with its sky taken off. If the workflow has <a href="/blocks/color/">Color</a>, the white balance of that block is applied. It also
    writes the covered pixels and the statistics of the stretch, measured once on the whole image at full resolution:
    median, median absolute deviation and the 99.995th percentile of the luminance and of each channel.</p>
    <p>Two repairs are made there. The periodic pattern of a colour mosaic is notched out of the image. The places where
    samples were clipped, the cores of the bright stars, take the colour of the light just around them, at the lowest
    brightness every channel allows. A combination of filters, like a single stack, is not cropped: the whole frame is
    shown and the user crops.</p>
    <p>Post then renders that image. The default rendering, with every slider at 2, is a midtones transfer function from a
    black point at the median minus 2.8 median absolute deviations to a white point at the 99.995th percentile, the median
    sky brought to a display level of 0.25. With linked channels the stretch is made on the luminance, so that the colour
    balance of the image is kept; without, each channel is stretched on its own, which neutralises a colour cast.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s2"><span class="secnum">2</span>Layers</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>Reading the panel.</b> Read from the bottom up, the layers panel is the order of the
    composition: a pixel layer, then its filters from the lowest to the highest, then the next row up.</aside>
    <p>A layer is one of three kinds.</p>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>A workflow layer</strong> is the image of a workflow: its Output block’s image, or one of the images of
      its <a href="/blocks/stars/">Stars/background</a> block (the starless image, the stars alone, or the two recombined). It is rendered by the
      engine from its base and its sliders (section 4).</li>
      <li><strong>An image layer</strong> is the result of any block (a stack, a gradient-removed or a denoised image) sent
      from Inspect. It is stretched automatically and moved by the Sky, Contrast and Saturation sliders. Its pixels are
      never saved in the composition, only the name of the result.</li>
      <li><strong>A filter layer</strong> is one filter (section 5). It applies to the one pixel layer it is nested under,
      or to everything below it when it is a row of its own.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>Each layer has an eye, an <strong>opacity</strong> from 0 to 100 % and a <strong>blend mode</strong>. The filters
    have them too. The modes are the following, a being what is below and b the layer.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 1.</b>The blend modes of a layer or a filter.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Mode</th><th>Result</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Normal</th><td>b</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Lighten</th><td>max(a, b)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Screen</th><td>1 − (1 − a)(1 − b)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Linear dodge (add)</th><td>min(a + b, 1)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Multiply</th><td>a × b</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Soft light</th><td>(1 − 2b) a² + 2ab</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>The mode is applied first, then the opacity and the mask mix the result with what is below:
    below + (result − below) × opacity × mask.</p>
    <h3>Stars and background</h3>
    <p>A workflow with a Stars/background block comes in as one layer with two images, each with its own filters: the
    starless image, stretched with the statistics of the whole image so that, recombined, it looks like the Output
    block’s, and the stars alone. The stars are stretched on their own, black at 0 and the white and midtone of the whole
    image, and go back on top in <strong>Screen</strong>, or in Linear dodge (add) or Lighten. Stretching them less is the
    star reduction: none is needed on the recombined image. The stars of the layer are a group: the real stars and, when
    the workflow has the Synthetic stars block, the synthetic ones beside them, each with its eye and its mask. Each side
    may also have a mask of its own, applied to its image after its filters and before the two are put together.</p>
    <p>For a combination of filters made on starless images, the Combine layers block puts the stars of another workflow
    back on its image; the stars of a combination of stars are stretched with the statistics of the starless combination.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s3"><span class="secnum">3</span>Masks</h2>
    <p>Every layer and every filter may have a mask. A mask is made of parts, each added to what is there, subtracted from
    it or intersected with it. A mask shows as a red overlay on the image.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 2.</b>The parts of a mask.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Part</th><th>What it selects</th><th>Settings</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">H-alpha</th><td>The red excess of the image, the red minus the green (mostly Hα, with [N II] and [S II]), smoothed over 6 px, 0 at its median and 1 at its 97th percentile (measured on the whole field).</td><td>None</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Luminance range</th><td>The pixels whose luminance lies between two levels, with soft edges.</td><td>From 40 %, To 100 %</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Color range</th><td>The pixels near a hue, weighted by their saturation.</td><td>Hue 0° – 359°, width 40° (10° – 120°)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Linear gradient</th><td>A ramp across the image, from the top, bottom, left or right.</td><td>Position 50 %, softness 40 % (1 % – 100 %)</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Brush</th><td>What is painted, with strokes that can be taken back one at a time.</td><td>Size 60 px, hardness 50 %</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>Three settings act on the whole mask: <strong>Feather</strong> (0 to 60 px, blurs the edge), <strong>Density</strong>
    (0 to 100 %, the share of the layer that shows where the mask is white) and <strong>Invert</strong>. A brush added to
    an empty mask takes away from a full one: the layer stays shown everywhere and the first stroke hides.</p>
    <p>The luminance and colour parts read the image they are on. A mask part is computed once for given settings and
    reused, so that adding a part to a mask computes that part only.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s4"><span class="secnum">4</span>The simplified sliders</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>How a position is read.</b> At 2 a setting has the value of the layer’s base. At the other
    positions the table’s step from position 2 is added, or its ratio to position 2 multiplies the base. Between whole
    positions the value is linear, and it stays inside the setting’s bounds.</aside>
    <p>A workflow layer is rendered from a base, a named rendering of the project or the suggestion, moved by its
    sliders. Each slider runs from 0 to 4 and its position 2 is the base.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 3.</b>The sliders, and the setting each one moves, from position 0 to position 4, for the suggested base.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Slider</th><th>Group</th><th>Ends</th><th>Setting</th><th>0 → 2 → 4</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sky</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>darker, brighter</td><td>Sky brightness</td><td class="n">0.15 → 0.25 → 0.37</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Contrast</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>softer, stronger</td><td>S-curve</td><td class="n">−0.35 → 0 → 0.5</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Faint tones</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>darker, brighter</td><td>Faint tones</td><td class="n">−0.8 → 0 → 1.6</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Saturation</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>paler, more vivid</td><td>Saturation</td><td class="n">0.6 → 1.2 → 1.85</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Colours</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>faded, true</td><td>Keep the colours</td><td class="n">0 → 0.5 → 1</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">H-alpha</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>less, more</td><td>H-alpha</td><td class="n">−0.6 → 0 → 2</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Noise smoothing</th><td>Nebula and background</td><td>less, more</td><td>Denoised share; faint area smoothing</td><td class="n">0.4 → 1 → 1; 0 → 0 → 1.6 px</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Strength</th><td>Stars</td><td>subtler, bolder</td><td>Star strength</td><td class="n">0.25 → 0.6 → 1.6</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Small stars</th><td>Stars</td><td>less, more</td><td>Small stars</td><td class="n">1 → 0 → 0</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Star size</th><td>Stars</td><td>smaller, larger</td><td>Star size</td><td class="n">−3 → −1 → 1 px</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Star color</th><td>Stars</td><td>whiter, more colorful</td><td>Star color</td><td class="n">0.4 → 1.2 → 2.3</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p><strong>Faint tones</strong> raises or lowers the tones just above the sky without moving the sky: a generalised
    hyperbolic stretch<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> of the range above the sky, its black point and symmetry point
    at the sky, applied to the luminance with the colour ratios kept. <strong>Colours</strong> sets how much of the stretch
    keeps the colours: at 0 each channel goes through the same curve, so that the faint colours turn grey and the bright
    cores white; at 1 the luminance is stretched and the three channels scaled with it, which keeps the colours of the linear
    image.<a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> The base is 0.5, so that nebulae keep their colour and the bright cores their
    structure. <strong>H-alpha</strong> adds the measured H-alpha emission to the red before the stretch, or takes it out;
    it is the red above a continuum predicted from the green (mostly Hα, with [N II] and [S II]), the red-to-green ratio
    of that continuum measured on the stars, as the continuum of a narrow-band Hα image is scaled on the
    stars.<a class="cite" href="#ref-3">[3]</a>
    <strong>Noise smoothing</strong> mixes the raw stack back into the denoised one below 2, and smooths the faint areas
    above, the bright ones keeping full resolution.</p>
    <p>A slider that a layer cannot offer is greyed, and its reason is shown: the star sliders work on the stars of the
    <a href="/blocks/stars/">Stars/background</a> block; the stars layer holds the stars only; noise smoothing needs the <a href="/blocks/denoise/">Denoise</a> block and works on the
    whole image; H-alpha is for colour cameras only, since in a palette combination H-alpha is its own channel and on a
    monochrome image there is no red over a continuum.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s5"><span class="secnum">5</span>Filters</h2>
    <aside class="note"><b>At their defaults.</b> Every filter is the identity at its defaults: adding one changes nothing
    until a setting is moved, and the composition keeps the image bit for bit.</aside>
    <p>A filter is a layer of its own kind. Dropped onto a pixel layer it is nested under it and acts on it alone; between
    two rows it acts on everything below. Each has an eye, an opacity, a blend mode and a mask. Switching it off, changing
    it or removing it leaves every other filter as it is.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 4.</b>The filters of Post, and the method each follows.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Family</th><th>Filter</th><th>Method</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row" rowspan="5">Stretch</th><td>Generalised hyperbolic stretch</td><td>Payne and Cranfield: a strong stretch around a chosen level, the shadows and highlights protected.<a class="cite" href="#ref-1">[1]</a> Factor ln(D + 1) 0 – 4, local intensity −5 – 15, symmetry point, shadow and highlight protection, black point.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Arcsinh stretch</td><td>pixel = x′ · asinh(L′ · stretch) / (L′ · asinh(stretch)), the colours kept.<a class="cite" href="#ref-2">[2]</a> Stretch 0 – 40, black point 0 – 0.2.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Histogram transformation</td><td>Shadows, midtones and highlights, through the midtones transfer function (m − 1) x / ((2m − 1) x − m).</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Curves</td><td>One curve per channel (RGB/K, R, G, B, CIE L*, saturation, chroma, hue), 2 to 100 control points, Akima, natural cubic or linear interpolation.<a class="cite" href="#ref-4">[4]</a> Presets, the user’s own, and .acv files.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Levels</td><td>Black point 0 – 0.3, white point 0.7 – 1, gamma 0.5 – 2.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" rowspan="4">Contrast</th><td>Local contrast</td><td>A large-radius unsharp mask: strength 0 – 1.5, scale 5 – 120 px.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Dehaze</td><td>The veil is measured as the background of a source extractor (meshes, 3σ clipping, mode, bicubic map)<a class="cite" href="#ref-5">[5]</a> and taken off with a haze model, the white kept.<a class="cite" href="#ref-6">[6]</a> Amount, scale 32 – 512 px.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Local histogram equalization</td><td>CLAHE on the CIE L* lightness.<a class="cite" href="#ref-7">[7]</a> Amount 0 – 1, contrast limit 1 – 8, kernel radius 16 – 512 px.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Highlight compression</td><td>A base and its detail by the guided filter;<a class="cite" href="#ref-8">[8]</a> the base is compressed above a knee, the detail kept. Strength, start 0.2 – 0.9, detail kept, scale 8 – 120 px.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" rowspan="4">Color</th><td>Saturation</td><td>Saturation around the luminance, −100 to +150 %, by hue range, with a background protection that spares the noise of the sky.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Colour mixer</td><td>Hue, saturation and luminance of eight colour ranges (red, orange, yellow, green, aqua, blue, purple, magenta) in CIE L*c*h*. The grey background and the white star cores are left alone.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Green removal</td><td>The green held under a neutral made of red and blue; four protections, amount, preserve lightness.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Color balance</td><td>Gains on red and blue (temperature) and on green (tint).</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" rowspan="3">Detail</th><td>Sharpen</td><td>Unsharp mask: strength 0 – 2, radius 0.5 – 4 px.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Multiscale sharpening</td><td>Starlet wavelet layers (B3 spline à trous) of 1, 2, 4, 8 and 16 px, each raised or lowered on its own, on the lightness or on each channel.<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">[9]</a></td></tr>
          <tr><td>Spot healing</td><td>A spot replaced by a patch of its neighbourhood and blended in by Poisson editing.<a class="cite" href="#ref-10">[10]</a> The source is found automatically, 2.2 to 3.2 radii away, by the patch search of PatchMatch,<a class="cite" href="#ref-11">[11]</a> and can be moved.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row" rowspan="3">Noise</th><td>Noise reduction</td><td>A Gaussian smoothing of the luminance and of the colour.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Multiscale noise reduction</td><td>Soft thresholding of the starlet layers,<a class="cite" href="#ref-9">[9]</a> each shrunk by k σ with σ estimated from the first layer; on the lightness, the chrominance or each channel.</td></tr>
          <tr><td>Faint area smoothing</td><td>Smooths the areas fainter than a level (0.05 – 0.8), the brighter ones untouched, so that the faint extensions can be raised without their grain.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Combine</th><td>H-alpha to red</td><td>The engine’s H-alpha map added to the red channel.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s6"><span class="secnum">6</span>Versions</h2>
    <p>A project keeps several versions of the same edit, each with its own layers, filters, masks and its own Undo and Redo.
    A click on the version’s name opens the list and a double-click renames it. A name must be given and must be
    unique. New version makes a copy of the one shown, and a version can be deleted while another remains. A deleted
    version can be brought back with Ctrl+Z. Layers copied into a new version keep the same settings, so a version’s
    switch reuses what the other computed. The exported files carry the version’s name.</p>
    <p>Nothing in Post renders in the interface thread. While a slider or the brush moves, a draft is shown (an eighth of
    the image on the CPU, a quarter on a graphics card); on release the quarter, then the half-resolution image of the screen; and,
    zoomed in, the visible region at full resolution, with a margin of 64 px so that the filters see their neighbours. A
    newer request replaces an older one.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s7"><span class="secnum">7</span>Export</h2>
    <p>Export writes the whole composition at full resolution, each layer rendered on the whole grid. The files go to the
    folder chosen and nowhere else; a folder inside the project’s results is refused. Each file is written under a
    temporary name and takes its final one in a single step: a failure leaves the earlier file as it was.</p>

    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 5.</b>The export formats. All are in sRGB, with the profile embedded where the format allows.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Format</th><th>File</th><th>Notes</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">TIFF 16-bit</th><td>name_16bit.tif</td><td>The default. Zlib compression.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">TIFF 32-bit float</th><td>name_32bit_float.tif</td><td>Zlib compression.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">PNG 16-bit</th><td>name.png</td><td></td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">JPEG</th><td>name.jpg</td><td>Quality 93 by default (50 – 100), chroma not subsampled (4:4:4): the colour of a star is not smeared.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">FITS 32-bit float</th><td>name.fits</td><td>With the astrometric solution (WCS) and the processing history in its header, and the target, the dates and the total exposure when known.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>

    <p>The size is the full resolution (the drizzled grid) or a long side of 2048 or 4096 px, reduced by area averaging.
    A quarter turn or a vertical mirror is applied without any resampling; the solution in a FITS header follows the turn.
    The export can add a history file next to the image, each layer as a separate file, the masks, the night card, and the
    sky grid and object names drawn into the picture.</p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s8"><span class="secnum">8</span>Settings</h2>
    <div class="tbl-wrap wide">
      <table class="tbl">
        <caption><b>Table 6.</b>The settings of the Output block and of a mask.</caption>
        <thead><tr><th>Setting</th><th>Value</th><th>Range</th><th>What it does</th></tr></thead>
        <tbody>
          <tr><th scope="row">Stretch</th><td>auto</td><td>auto</td><td>How the linear image is brought to the screen: black just below the sky, the sky at a set brightness.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Linked channels</th><td>on</td><td>on, off</td><td>One stretch for the three channels, which keeps the colour balance; off, each channel is stretched on its own.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Sky brightness</th><td class="n">0.25</td><td class="n">0.02 – 0.6</td><td>Display level of the median sky after the stretch.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Saturation</th><td class="n">1.2</td><td class="n">0 – 3</td><td>Colour saturation around the luminance, after the stretch.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Keep the colours</th><td class="n">0.5</td><td class="n">0 – 1</td><td>How much of the stretch keeps the colours.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Faint tones</th><td class="n">0</td><td class="n">−1 – 3</td><td>Raises or lowers the faint tones above the sky.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Feather</th><td class="n">0 px</td><td class="n">0 – 60</td><td>Blurs the edge of a mask.</td></tr>
          <tr><th scope="row">Density</th><td class="n">100 %</td><td class="n">0 – 100</td><td>The share of the layer that shows where the mask is white.</td></tr>
        </tbody>
      </table>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="s9"><span class="secnum">9</span>Limits, and what was tried</h2>
    <ul>
      <li><strong>Post rendering is slow on integrated AMD graphics.</strong> Tested on Windows 11 with an RTX 4060 Ti, an
      RTX 3070 and a laptop without an NVIDIA card. When the memory of the graphics card is full, the layer is rendered
      again on the CPU, and a note says so.</li>
      <li><strong>The Output stretch is automatic only.</strong> The other stretches are filters on a layer, not a setting
      of the Output block.</li>
      <li><strong>Layers pulled from <a href="/blocks/stacking/">Stacking</a> leave the graph early.</strong> A layer taken from a block before <a href="/blocks/denoise/">Denoise</a> has no
      noise slider, and one taken before <a href="/blocks/stars/">Stars/background</a> has no star sliders; each says why.</li>
      <li><strong>Without <a href="/blocks/color/">Color</a>, the colours are those of the camera.</strong> Each channel’s sky is made grey and scaled to
      the same spread, so that the colour cast of the light pollution does not tint the preview; the gradient stays visible,
      since removing it is the <a href="/blocks/gradient/">Gradient</a> block’s job.</li>
      <li><strong>Not in this version.</strong> Colour profiles other than sRGB, and the linear outputs.</li>
      <li><strong>The first Post’s arcsinh stretch around a symmetry point</strong> is hidden from the filter menu and
      kept only so that the compositions that hold it still open.</li>
    </ul>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2 id="refs">References</h2>
